<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Bowling Tracker"
  description="A private mobile bowling tracker for quick scoring, equipment and pattern context, practice drills, and evidence-based analytics."
  eyebrow="Sports product · Personal analytics"
  lede="A final score tells me what I shot. It does not tell me why. Bowling Tracker keeps the useful context—ball, lane, pattern, practice, league, adjustments—without turning every trip to the lanes into a data-entry job."
  status="Active development"
  access="Private repository · Public-safe case study"
  tags={['Flutter', 'Offline sync', 'Analytics', 'Domain rules']}
  sections={['The problem', 'Capture without slowing the game', 'Rules before dashboards', 'What the history can answer', 'Why it stays private']}
  tone="slate"
  visual="bowling"
>
  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A score sheet records the outcome but leaves out the conditions. To learn whether a change helped, I need to know which ball I used, what lane and oil pattern I saw, whether I was practicing or competing, and which leaves kept showing up. The app has to collect that evidence while I am bowling, not demand a spreadsheet afterward.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Capture without slowing the game</h2>
    <p>
      The Flutter app supports a fast score-entry path and more detailed pin-level entry when I want it. The domain model runs from session to series to game to delivery. Ball attribution belongs to the delivery, because switching equipment mid-game should not make the analysis guess. A local Drift/SQLite store handles the lane-side experience; synchronization can catch up when connectivity returns.
    </p>
    <p>
      Equipment has a lifecycle instead of a delete button that erases history. A retired ball still explains old sessions. Maintenance entries record cleaning, polishing, resurfacing, and other changes so a performance shift has context.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Rules before dashboards</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Scoring and handicap rules live in domain services rather than being reconstructed by each screen.</li>
      <li>Statistics are calculated from completed games; a social session can stay in history without skewing competitive averages.</li>
      <li>Derived averages and records come from source events rather than stored totals that can drift from the throws.</li>
      <li>A house pattern is a useful grouping, not a fabricated named oil pattern.</li>
      <li>Spare drills track attempts and per-leave conversion, turning practice into something more useful than “felt better today.”</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="case-callout">
    <h2>What the history can answer</h2>
    <p>
      The repository includes session history, personal records, rolling averages, per-ball and oil-category views, monthly trends, alley comparisons, leave conversion, and a pin-leave heat map. Those views share the same underlying deliveries and completed-game rules. A chart is worth keeping only if it helps me choose a ball, target a drill, or understand a change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why it stays private</h2>
    <p>
      This is a mobile-first product still under development. The public case study describes the scoring model, offline strategy, and product decisions without publishing the repository or anyone's personal performance records.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
